Local sandbox
=============

This box has no route off itself. Stripe, Influx, Auth0 and Amazon are all
unreachable. What the backend would send to Amazon is answered by an emulator
listening on http://127.0.0.1:4566, which speaks the same wire protocols the
AWS SDK uses; AWS_ENDPOINT_URL is already exported, so clients constructed the
way the project constructs them land there with no code change.

    platform account   647662420899
    region             us-east-1
    STAGE              dev
    INFLUX_ORG         meteringco

Because Stripe cannot be reached, the helper this project uses to put a
customer on the payment rail -- CustomerEntity.createStripeCustomer in
src/customer/entities/customer.entity.ts -- is stood in for when the service is
exercised. Code that reaches Stripe by some other route will simply hang.

The archive
-----------

The business this sandbox serves is biz_apex. Last month's customer
provisioning for it was archived object by object and is still there:

    aws s3 ls s3://meteringco-config-archive-dev/customer-provisioning/2026-08/
    aws s3 cp s3://meteringco-config-archive-dev/customer-provisioning/2026-08/manual-with-a-free-trial.ndjson -

or, without the SDK,

    curl -s http://127.0.0.1:4566/meteringco-config-archive-dev/customer-provisioning/2026-08/card-on-a-connected-account.ndjson

Each object is one onboarding: the request that came in, the state it met on
the way through -- the caller's customer allowance, the ids the business was
already using, its settings row, the contract that came back for the offering
named, whether metering was up -- and, under `outcome`, what came back out of
the service. `influxWrites` is the configuration row it persisted, in line
protocol. `webhookEvents` is what subscribers were told. `auditEvents` is what
was recorded when something went wrong. `response` is what the caller got back,
or `error` if it was turned away.

It is a thin month. Six onboardings survive retention, one for each shape the
platform bothers to keep: an exhausted allowance, an identifier already in use,
a card customer on a business that never connected its payment account, a card
customer the platform opened a rail customer for, a card customer that brought
its own, and a manual customer on a contract carrying a free trial. What the
service asked of Stripe, Influx, the contract service and the token consumer on
the way is not kept -- only what it persisted, announced, recorded and returned.
Real months carry far more of all of it, so treat this as a sample rather than
the shape of the world.

Running things
--------------

TypeScript in this project can be run directly with tsx, for example

    tsx -e "import('./src/customer/customer.service.js').then(m => console.log(Object.keys(m)))"

To point the emulator at a different document, restart it yourself:

    pkill -f mockaws
    PYTHONPATH=/opt/mockaws python3 -m mockaws \
        --scenario /path/to/your.json --host 127.0.0.1 --port 4566 --seed 7 &
